1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,395
This is a living network.

2
00:00:03,435 --> 00:00:08,018
Neurons from a rat brain, grown on
a chip in four linked compartments,

3
00:00:08,058 --> 00:00:11,138
fire together, fall
silent, and fire again.

4
00:00:11,178 --> 00:00:15,992
Every mark is a real spike, heard
by an electrode beneath the cells.

5
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:19,365
Networks like this let
us ask a hard question:

6
00:00:19,405 --> 00:00:23,342
does a chemical disturb a brain
while it is still being built?

7
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:29,598
Today, the answer is slow and costly.

8
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:32,899
In the US EPA's network formation assay,

9
00:00:32,939 --> 00:00:39,253
212 of 243 chemicals were tested at
seven concentrations, in replicate wells,

10
00:00:39,293 --> 00:00:43,576
and followed for twelve
days as the network grew.

11
00:00:43,616 --> 00:00:45,873
Each compound is analysed on its own,

12
00:00:45,913 --> 00:00:49,764
and nothing learned from the
last one helps with the next.

13
00:00:50,483 --> 00:00:54,466
NeuroChip Twin treats each
chemical as a few-shot task.

14
00:00:54,506 --> 00:01:01,424
Its core, NeuroTrajectory, is a neural
process meta-learned across 243 chemicals.

15
00:01:01,464 --> 00:01:05,884
Give it three measured concentrations
of a compound it has never seen,

16
00:01:05,924 --> 00:01:08,170
and it returns the whole response:

17
00:01:08,210 --> 00:01:13,833
all seventeen network features, at every
concentration and every recording day,

18
00:01:13,873 --> 00:01:16,837
with a calibrated band of uncertainty.

19
00:01:16,877 --> 00:01:19,401
And when it is unsure, it says so.

20
00:01:20,417 --> 00:01:22,838
Here is the system at work.

21
00:01:22,878 --> 00:01:25,409
The demo chemical follows a rule:

22
00:01:25,449 --> 00:01:30,186
DDT, the median-gain chemical
of Figure 4 in our report.

23
00:01:30,226 --> 00:01:32,260
The model never saw it.

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,270
We enter three measured concentrations,

25
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:41,569
and in seconds the twin forecasts every
other dose and day, inside a 90% band.

26
00:01:41,609 --> 00:01:44,145
Then we reveal the real wells.

27
00:01:44,185 --> 00:01:49,048
The abstention flag stays
clear, and the error is 0.61,

28
00:01:49,088 --> 00:01:52,102
against 0.90 for interpolation.

29
00:01:53,417 --> 00:01:55,414
Then come the decisions.

30
00:01:55,454 --> 00:01:56,630
Hazard call:

31
00:01:56,670 --> 00:02:01,024
a developmental neurotoxicant,
matching its reference label.

32
00:02:01,064 --> 00:02:04,619
The DIV 7 gate says keep recording:

33
00:02:04,659 --> 00:02:07,465
this network is already changing.

34
00:02:07,505 --> 00:02:12,425
And with the top dose measured first,
DoseCompass picks the lowest dose next.

35
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:15,493
Across all 243 chemicals,

36
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:21,973
forecasts from three doses have 11%
lower error than the best baseline.

37
00:02:22,013 --> 00:02:26,823
Nominal 90% intervals cover 89.9%.

38
00:02:26,863 --> 00:02:32,167
From three concentrations, the hazard
call finds 65 known neurotoxicants;

39
00:02:32,207 --> 00:02:37,626
EPA's rules, using every
concentration, find 52 to 56.

40
00:02:37,666 --> 00:02:41,224
That is 57% fewer treated wells.

41
00:02:41,264 --> 00:02:46,644
At DIV 7, 26% of chemicals
can stop recording.

42
00:02:46,684 --> 00:02:50,156
DoseCompass only ties a log-spaced design,

43
00:02:50,196 --> 00:02:56,549
but measuring the top dose first lifts
activity detection from 88 to 91%.

44
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:01,841
The same readout lives on
compartmentalised chips.

45
00:03:01,881 --> 00:03:06,143
On a public four-compartment
chip with 440 sorted axons,

46
00:03:06,183 --> 00:03:09,644
the chip layer learns
directed connectivity:

47
00:03:09,684 --> 00:03:13,104
how much activity flows
forward, and how fast.

48
00:03:13,144 --> 00:03:20,390
Axon direction checks out
for 437/437 measurable axons.

49
00:03:20,430 --> 00:03:24,791
Bursts that coincide, we read
as synchrony, not propagation.

50
00:03:25,517 --> 00:03:28,204
What we do not claim matters as much.

51
00:03:28,244 --> 00:03:31,961
These are rat cortical
cultures, not human tissue.

52
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:37,316
Dose planning was tested retrospectively,
on wells already recorded.

53
00:03:37,356 --> 00:03:43,383
Model-derived potency lost to plain
interpolation, so we report interpolation.

54
00:03:43,423 --> 00:03:48,667
And with only 19 negative chemicals,
the hazard comparison is underpowered:

55
00:03:48,707 --> 00:03:52,473
we claim no superiority over EPA's rules.

56
00:03:53,383 --> 00:03:58,480
For a neural organ-on-a-chip lab, this
means fewer wells, earlier decisions,

57
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:02,545
and recordings that become a
reusable, predictive asset.

58
00:04:02,585 --> 00:04:07,226
Map your chips to our schema, anchor
on the top dose, forecast the rest,

59
00:04:07,266 --> 00:04:12,122
stop early where it is safe, and let
every experiment improve the next.

60
00:04:12,162 --> 00:04:13,358
The next step:

61
00:04:13,398 --> 00:04:16,716
a prospective test on
a sponsor's own chips.

62
00:04:17,417 --> 00:04:20,676
From a few wells, the
whole developing network.

63
00:04:20,716 --> 00:04:22,366
NeuroChip Twin.

64
00:04:22,406 --> 00:04:26,283
The code, the report and the
live demo are listed here.
